[{:seon.task/name "the-wiki-loads-at-boot"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.root/wiki-tx` loads the wiki (`seon.doc/load-dir-tx` over the checkout's docs/) onto main in the boot fact's own commit, after the program facts so cited names resolve, and before any agent forks; an unchanged wiki writes nothing. A refused load (a link to no section) is a recorded :platform error naming the link (`my.root/problems` shows it), main keeps the wiki it held, and the boot goes on (`seon.root-test/a-refused-wiki-is-a-recorded-problem-and-the-boot-goes-on`). The declared proof `seon.doc-test/a-booted-root-holds-the-wiki` is not written yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/a-booted-root-holds-the-wiki}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the wiki's sections on main (`seon.doc/load-tx`), each with the references its text cites (`:seon.doc/references`, read by `seon.ref/parse`); there is no docs check yet. Correct: `bin/check docs` is queries over the docs facts, run in every merged-result check, over every page under docs/ plus docs/README.md, AGENTS.md and BAGGAGE.md. Red: a link or `path#anchor` that reaches nothing; a cited path that names no file; any other reference that does not resolve; a stale section, one citing a var at doc or code depth whose defining form differs from the one at the page's last commit (both read with the reader, no text diff). Health is reported first, then red, by these measures (from jolt/patterns 2da2255, `git show 2da2255`, re-keyed from its Describes lines onto code-depth citations): :two-homes (two sections citing one var at code depth: the sections whose `:seon.doc/references` hold the var, filtered by the depth `seon.ref/parse` reads from their text), :no-lead, :lead-echoes-heading (the lead's words a subset of the heading's), :lead-too-long (over 40 words), :lead-cites (file:line in the lead), :heading-has-code (a code span or file:line in a heading), :orphan (no link or parent edge reaches it from docs/README.md; a page's sections are reached through the page; README's groups and same-page anchors count as links), :isolated-page (links nowhere), :changelog (a dated heading, or two or more lines starting with a date; a date in prose is not one). Baseline on jolt/wiki's tree: two-homes 20, no-lead 32, lead-cites 18, lead-too-long 5, heading-has-code 14, orphan 13, isolated-page 3. jolt/patterns' three fixture deftests (a-var-with-two-homes-is-duplication-and-names-the-home, a-lead-that-is-missing-echoes-its-heading-runs-long-or-cites-is-flagged, a-heading-with-code-an-orphan-an-isolated-page-and-a-changelog-are-flagged) port with code-depth citations in place of Describes lines. The style is written once, in docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/an-unresolved-reference-is-red
                      seon.doc-test/a-section-older-than-what-it-cites-is-stale
                      seon.doc-test/two-code-depth-homes-are-flagged
                      seon.doc-test/an-orphan-is-flagged}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-concept-has-its-page"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/README.md is the map and states how the wiki is kept (docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept: one home per concept, the section citing it at code depth; a standalone lead; everything linked; current truth). 19 pages, among them agent, tasks, cost, merging, machine, storage, render, source, references and failure-points. References are what text cites (`seon.ref/parse`, docs/references.md); there are no declared reference keys. Still without a page: root's procs (the system), the checks (bin/check) and the server, whose op table lives in docs/parity.md#the-mcp-server; a task citing them pulls nothing. Source text: jolt/wiki (8da07b4..11f34a1) wrote twelve concept pages, a day older than main and partly untrue on it (`git show jolt/wiki:docs/<page>.md`); main took its rules (README) and rejected its declared keys. Correct, each section verified against main's code before it is written: new pages docs/system.md (one section per proc in resources/seon/system.edn; its idioms stay homed in docs/flow.md and are linked), docs/check.md (bin/check as it runs now; steps as tasks is `seon.reconcile/a-check-step-is-a-task`) and a server page holding the server's one op table; jolt/wiki's store.md folded into docs/storage.md, printer.md and context.md into docs/render.md, program.md into docs/source.md, worktrees.md into docs/merging.md and docs/machine.md, and wiki.md's section shape (plain stable headings with no code, file:line or date; a lead of at most 40 words with no citation; file:line only inside a function) into docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept. Every page on the map and linking its neighbours, each var's one home citing it at code depth, docs/vocabulary.md linking each word to its section; nothing dated (no session ids, t values, pids, today/still/now). Not applied: Describes lines, `:one`/`:owner`/`:migrate`, `:seon.task/docs`, `:seon.task/refs`, `:seon/docs`, `:pattern-of`, `seon.doc/reached`. Replaces the knowledge held only in AGENTS.md and docstrings for these concepts (AGENTS.md links the pages); retires jolt/wiki and jolt/patterns-wiki-proof. Deletes no code."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/the-wiki-loads-with-every-link-resolved
                      seon.doc-test/every-page-is-reached-from-the-map
                      seon.doc-test/every-section-leads-with-a-sentence-of-at-most-40-words}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: nothing detects \"a check that cannot fail\" (AGENTS.md §9) or a proof \"a stub would pass\" (§5). Correct: for each task's proofs, and each definition a proof reaches directly, run the proof against three stubs of that definition: (constantly nil), identity, and its first argument unchanged. A proof still green against a stub does not test that definition: a placebo, recorded as a problem fact (class :proof, naming the proof, the definition and the stub that passed). The stub is a real definition in a forked test environment holding exactly the definitions the run wants (never with-redefs or alter-var-root), on the fork path of `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`. Cost: proofs x directly reached definitions x 3 forked runs. The idea is writ's spec self-check, which rejects a law any implementation satisfies (the archived paper option3-ECOSYSTEM.md (git history); a first cut on jolt/patterns 35e1208). It would flag jolt/patterns' idiom proofs that exercised step-fns defined in the test file itself."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo seon.proofs-test/a-real-proof-fails-against-every-stub}}
 {:seon.task/name "names-are-clojures-words"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (DECISIONS: Names are Clojure's, its libraries' or Jolt's; AGENTS.md §7a): pass 1 is merged. Old house words in names became Clojure's, a library's or Jolt's, by git mv and exact-token sed, one commit per word:
- door, entrance -> server: `seon.server` (`seon.server/ops`, a map of :op -> fn; error type :seon.server/defect); the MCP tools keep their seon_* names;
- verb -> function (by its var), or op where it is a request type;
- lane -> worktree session: bin/worktrees, bin/worktree-check (verdict WORKTREE-CHECK); `seon.fx`'s performer procs are workers (`seon.fx/worker-proc`);
- land, landing -> merge: docs/merging.md;
- dragon -> failure point: docs/failure-points.md; bin/laws lists the sites of four laws (catch, clocks, measures, resolution);
- census -> mg/check: `seon.check/mg-check`, bin/check mode mg-check;
- admit, guarantees (the contract sense) -> instrument: `seon.check/instrument`; `seon.fx/admit` is admission by workload and keeps its name;
- pillar -> principle; fence -> guard (a Markdown code fence, CommonMark's word, stays in `seon.reader` and `seon.ref`);
- merge train -> octopus merge (`seon.writer/octopus-merge`);
- harvest -> stage (`seon.stage/stage!`);
- stand-in -> proc (`seon.agent-proc`); the scripted model is \"the scripted model\";
- dial -> config key (`seon.reconcile/merge-limits`).
Stored names are pass 2 (`seon.db/stored-attributes-take-the-new-words`): task ids and task file names (seon.fences.edn, seon.entrance.edn, merge-trains-off-as-decided, the-dragon-gate-sees-fences, a-door-defect-answers-that-request-alone, a-train-is-proven-by-one-combined-run, with-merge-census-cost), `:seon.config.root/entrance-port`, `:seon.config.contract/guarantees`, `:seon.ai.model/thinking-dials`, and refusal with its stored keyword. waste stays `:seon.run/waste`; stuck goes with the budget (DECISIONS: Owner answers, 2026-10-23 morning). Archived notes keep their text. Proof that stays: `seon.doc-test/no-name-uses-an-old-word` reads AGENTS.md §7a's table and finds no namespace, var, check mode, bin script or docs page named with an old word (names from the compiler's facts, all-ns and ns-interns, not a regex over code; the kept senses listed in the test as data, each with its reason)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/no-name-uses-an-old-word}}
 {:seon.task/name "docs-are-the-only-documentation"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: \"Can we switch to only docs in docs and we just keep it up to date instead of separate notes?\" and \"If it's in the docs it's linkable from any task right?\" (yes: a docs section has an address `path#anchor` and `seon.ref` pulls it at the depth a citation asks, docs/references.md; a notes file is only a path). What exists: docs/ is the wiki (docs/README.md, how it is kept); a scratch directory held dated write-ups (audits, assessments, research, the printer-eval scores, the self-contained plan) and the scratch paper archive (git history) their predecessors; about 25 citations in src docstrings, tests and task plans point at the archived paper option3-*.md (git history) and other notes files. Correct: every lasting fact of a note is a docs section where its problem lives (one home per concept), cited from the task or docstring that needs it as `docs/page.md#section`; the note is then removed and git history keeps it (the prototype's notes are the tag history/prototype once the self-contained plan's step D has run; `git ls-tree -r --name-only history/prototype` lists them). Findings from now on go into the task or the section, never a scratch file (AGENTS.md section 3 says this already; make it a rule of docs/README.md). Per note: read it, write the section or find the one that has it, repoint every citation, delete it. The citations are src and test edits, so one bin/prove at the end. Not done by the self-contained plan: it leaves the scratch directory as it is and excludes it from its grep. Order: after the self-contained plan's steps C to E have merged, so the archive copy it makes is not needed (do not copy the scratch paper archive (git history) into next at all)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "any-markdown-file-is-docs-it-writes-back"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-01: \"Docs are like code, we import them and we have a plan for writing them back to their files if we are happy\"; \"I want docs updates as agents are working and them treating them not as files that are loading, but docs as part of the system\"; \"section is a terrible name lets swap that out for docs\"; \"I want to retain the tools for an agent to directly read from a markdown file with a filename and write to it too\"; \"any md file anywhere (full path) ideally. It's a general tool that we use internally to index and write back our own updates. So make it easy to use from our data structures\".\n\nWhat exists: docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store (one entity per heading of the wiki dirs' pages, seon.doc/load-tx and load-dir-tx at root's boot), a section a program key in the one three-way (docs/merging.md#seons-merge-is-a-three-way-merge-of-the-program), and the writer refusing a branch's own change as :seon.merge/unwritten (task seon.merge/what-merges-is-what-a-file-states, whose steps (a) to (d) this task carries out for any file).\n\nThe shape, one value for every use: a page is data, {:path p :docs [doc ...]}, each doc {:address :heading :level :text :docs [child ...]} in the page's order, the text the only thing written and everything else (lead, references, names, read) derived by the one parse as load-tx derives it. The same value is what my.doc/page answers, what my.doc/write! takes, what a cited page is under @ctx (so a swap! of it is the edit) and what the render draws; seon.doc/page-text of it is the file, byte for byte.\n\nSteps, in order: (1) rename :seon.doc/* to :seon.doc/* (36 files, 261 uses on 2026-11-01; the word section stays in prose for a page's part), with the boot's wiki digest covering the schema so a renamed store loads again (seon.root/wiki-digest reads pages, program and task ids only today). (2) the round-trip: store each doc's level and position, and the text before a page's first heading, so seon.doc/page-text of a loaded page is its file; proof seon.doc-test/load-then-page-text-is-a-zero-diff over every page of docs/ and AGENTS.md, as seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff is for src. (3) any path: my.doc/page and my.doc/write! of a full path (a path in the checkout stored relative to it, so worktrees and generations agree; any other absolute), loading that one page into the agent's branch and touching no other page (md-scan 29's one-page load swept 556 sections), write! taking the page value or its text and refusing as edit! refuses, with the parse's problem named; a plain spit into the checkout from an agent's REPL is refused naming write!. (4) write-back: a merge's changed pages are written as its changed namespaces are (seon.source/changed-files, seon.fx/git-commit on the merges branch); a file outside the repository is written by seon.fx/write-file when its text on disk is still the text it was loaded from, else it is an unmerged entry (base, ours, and the file as theirs); :seon.merge/unwritten goes. (5) readers read the branch's db, never the file (seon.problems/old-words reads AGENTS.md's file today). Then the fence bench task, parked on this.\n\nFound at root's REPL, 2026-11-01: (i) the store already holds a page byte for byte but for order: a page drawn from its sections, each heading at its depth (# per level), a blank line after it, its text, a blank line, is its file exactly for 28 of the 38 pages; the ten others differ by a doubled or missing blank line (render.md, source.md, health.md's trailing blank among them) or by the order the draw guessed. Children by entity id put docs/agent.md#working-a-task last, though it is the page's second heading, so the one new attribute is :seon.doc/position; level is the depth and needs no attribute, and the ten pages are made regular once. (ii) the renders need no new path: every part of #p-task but ctx-keys already answers data, a cite's model render (seon.render.ui/cite-model) sends a doc as {:seon.print/prose ...} Markdown and its html render (cite) draws the same text as HTML; a call vector [#'f arg] prints as the call, and a prose argument prints as its Markdown, while an eager (markdown text) prints its :div and :class hiccup. (iii) the natural edit is the one agents know: edit! of a doc's address with [[old new] ...] as of a var (seon.repl/edit!), the page then loaded by load-tx so the derived attributes follow; a swap! of :seon.doc/text through @ctx (seon.ctx/write!) would write the text and leave lead, references and names as they were, so it reaches load-tx the same way. The doc cite says how, beside its :read, as a schema cite says :change.\n\nBuilt on jolt/docs-any-md, 2026-11-01: the rename (seon.doc aliased doc: a base program analyzed beside the image keeps its own docs alias, bin/check health), :seon.doc/heading as written, /level and /position, seon.doc/markdown with seon.doc-test/a-loaded-page-draws-back-as-its-file over the whole wiki (seven pages made regular), and seon.repl/edit! of a doc's address (seon.doc/doc-source, edit-tx; docs/agent.md#changing-a-doc; seon.repl-test/a-doc-is-edited-as-a-definition-is). Left: step (3)'s any path and my.doc/write!, step (4) write-back, step (5).\n\nThe owner, 2026-11-01, next: \"we may need to refactor how we are indexing and the format so we have a better representation that isn't just the files parsed, but is the most useful way for us to piece docs together into context that will help the agent. I want the ability to curate and come up with targeted docs for whatever we need to teach the agents and to have the task templates be mostly great info off the shelf that an agent can customize.\" The sessions' proposal, from what exists (docs/references.md#an-entity-cites): (6) a doc's citations are :seon.cite entities of the doc, as a task's plan's are (seon.task/cites-tx), so one citation representation serves tasks, agents and docs, and :seon.doc/references, /names and /dead-links become reads of them; (7) a curated doc is a doc whose text is mostly citations at their depths: cited whole, its own cites come into the page one level down, as a task's needs' do, each once at the strongest depth any asks (seon.render.ui/page-cites); (8) each kind's instructions in seon.template/instructions become a doc (a templates page), a task made citing its kind's template as an agent is made citing :seon.config.agent/cites (seon.task/agent-cites-tx), so the agent customizes it with my.agent/cite! and uncite!, edits its plan, and edit!s the template doc itself when the change is general; (9) the vaguer-task step: a task whose plan cites little, its first step finding and citing its references, which the person sees in the side list."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/load-then-page-text-is-a-zero-diff seon.doc-test/a-page-is-read-and-written-by-its-path}}]
